Body:
Confirming this on a self-hosted OpenAI-compatible gateway/router, configured as a custom provider that lives only in `opencode.json` and uses `@ai-sdk/openai-compatible`. Two paths hit the same capability gate:

1. Attaching/pasting an image is rewritten to a `[Attached image/png: …]` placeholder.
2. Reading a local image via the Read tool returns `ERROR: Cannot read "image.png" (this model does not support image input)`.

Minimal repro:

```jsonc
{
  "provider": {
    "mygateway": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "options": { "baseURL": "https://gateway.example.com/v1" },
      "models": {
        "my-vision-alias": { "name": "my-vision-alias" }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

The upstream route is vision-capable: sending the exact same image directly to the gateway as a base64 `image_url` data URI via `curl` works and the model describes it correctly. So this is client-side capability resolution, not the model or the gateway — the custom model's `capabilities.input` resolves to `["text"]` (no models.dev entry, and the gateway's `/v1/models` carries no modality metadata), so `image/*` parts are dropped before they reach the already-functional OpenAI-compatible conversion.

Workaround that works today — declare modalities explicitly per model:

```jsonc
"my-vision-alias": {
  "name": "my-vision-alias",
  "attachment": true,
  "modalities": { "input": ["text", "image"], "output": ["text"] }
}
```

Related: #44639 (same root cause plus the misleading error), #20802, and the proposed defaults in #21627 / #23501. Flipping the default for custom `@ai-sdk/openai-compatible` models would remove the need for per-model config that most users won't discover.

